WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.940
Lambda workloads must be designed for duplicate events, retries, and partial failures.

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:11.880
AWS recommends writing idempotent function code so duplicate events are handled safely.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:22.060
This matters because event delivery behavior can repeat work after timeouts, temporary failures, queue visibility changes, or stream processing interruptions.

4
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:33.700
Idempotent design can use natural uniqueness, idempotency keys, conditional writes, deduplication tables, checkpointing, or trusted server-side state where appropriate.

5
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.040
The goal is for repeated processing of the same logical work item to produce a safe and predictable result.

6
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:48.240
Asynchronous invocations, queue event source mappings, stream sources, and API-style invocations have different retry and failure behavior.

7
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:57.540
Developers should know which system retries, how long retry attempts continue, what happens after repeated failures, and which errors should be treated as permanent.

8
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:03.180
SQS and stream batches need partial failure and poison-message review.

9
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.440
A single bad item should not indefinitely hold back unrelated items when the platform supports partial batch responses.

10
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:17.140
Ordering expectations, visibility timeouts, maximum receive counts, and redrive behavior should be designed together.

11
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:26.700
Dead-letter queues, destinations, redrive policies, visibility timeouts, retry limits, and alerting should be planned as a system.

12
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:40.080
Side effects such as payments, emails, object writes, database changes, and external API calls need special review under retry conditions because repeated side effects can affect users and operations.
